---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import TopologyScene from '../components/TopologyScene.astro';

const capabilities = [
  ['Native Wayland client', 'Validated'],
  ['Persistent sessions & restore', 'Validated'],
  ['Panes, tabs & presets', 'Validated'],
  ['CLI, SSH & MCP automation', 'Validated'],
  ['GitHub release builds', 'Available'],
  ['Arch AUR packages', 'Available'],
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Splinterm — a native Wayland terminal with persistent sessions"
  description="Splinterm is a native Wayland terminal backed by a persistent daemon. Close the window; your shells, pane layouts, and scrollback keep running."
>
  <section class="intro-hero" aria-labelledby="intro-title">
    <img class="intro-mark" src="/logo-transparent.png" alt="" width="768" height="768" />
    <h1 id="intro-title" class="intro-wordmark">splinterm</h1>
    <p class="intro-status">Stable 0.2.0 · Arch &amp; Omarchy · Wayland</p>
    <p class="intro-thesis">A native Wayland terminal backed by a persistent daemon. Close the window; your shells keep running.</p>
    <div class="intro-actions" aria-label="Get started with Splinterm">
      <a class="button primary intro-primary" href="/docs/install/">Install Splinterm <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/docs/quickstart/">Read the quickstart <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
    <div class="intro-install" aria-label="Recommended installation command">
      <span>AUR · may include prereleases</span>
      <code><i aria-hidden="true">$</i> yay -S splinterm-bin</code>
      <a href="/docs/status/">Platform support</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="product" class="hero shell-section" aria-labelledby="product-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span>0.2.0</span> · Persistent by design</p>
      <h2 id="product-title">Your shells outlive their <em>window.</em></h2>
      <p class="hero-lede">
        Most terminals own the shells running inside them—close the window and everything stops.
        Splinterm moves process ownership into a background daemon called <code>splinterd</code>.
        Group projects into Lairs, arrange split layouts into Dojos, and let windows come and go.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/docs/concepts/">How the model works <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="#workflow">Try the workflow <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">Written in Rust, grounded in Foot’s terminal emulation, and built first for Omarchy and Arch Linux.</p>
    </div>
    <TopologyScene />
  </section>

  <section class="manifesto shell-section" aria-labelledby="persistence-title">
    <div class="section-index" aria-hidden="true">01 / Persistence</div>
    <div class="manifesto-copy">
      <h2 id="persistence-title">Built-in multiplexing, without nesting tmux.</h2>
      <p>
        <code>splinterd</code> holds your running shells, pane layouts, and scrollback in the
        background. A Lair groups a project, a Dojo holds a split layout, and each Splint is a
        single terminal pane. Closing a window simply detaches your view—nothing terminates unless
        you explicitly ask for it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="sequence" aria-label="Persistent session workflow">
      <li><span>01</span><strong>Open</strong><small>Launch a terminal and start working inside a persistent Lair.</small></li>
      <li><span>02</span><strong>Arrange</strong><small>Split panes into Dojos, open tabs, or launch a saved preset.</small></li>
      <li><span>03</span><strong>Return</strong><small>Close the window anytime and reattach to the exact same Dojo later.</small></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wayland-section shell-section" aria-labelledby="wayland-title">
    <div class="section-index" aria-hidden="true">02 / Native Wayland</div>
    <div class="wayland-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Direct compositor integration</p>
      <h2 id="wayland-title">Straight to Wayland, with persistence underneath.</h2>
      <p>
        Splinterm speaks Wayland directly instead of running through XWayland. Scaling, keyboard
        input, clipboard, IME, and frame timing live in a lightweight graphical client, while
        <code>splinterd</code> keeps your sessions alive below it.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/wayland/">Why native Wayland matters <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>

    <div class="wayland-path" aria-label="Splinterm presentation and persistence architecture">
      <div><span>01</span><small>Compositor</small><strong>Hyprland / Wayland</strong></div>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
      <div><span>02</span><small>Disposable view</small><strong>splinterm window</strong></div>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
      <div><span>03</span><small>Background daemon</small><strong>splinterd</strong></div>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
      <div><span>04</span><small>Running work</small><strong>shells · layouts · scrollback</strong></div>
    </div>

    <div class="wayland-benefits">
      <article><span>Scale</span><h3>Crisp across displays</h3><p>Follows compositor output geometry and fractional scaling directly, without XWayland blur.</p></article>
      <article><span>Input</span><h3>First-class text &amp; IME</h3><p>Native Wayland keyboard, pointer, clipboard, compose sequences, and inline IME preedit.</p></article>
      <article><span>Frames</span><h3>Damage-driven redraws</h3><p>Updates only the terminal regions that changed, timed cleanly to compositor frame callbacks.</p></article>
      <article><span>Lifecycle</span><h3>Disposable windows</h3><p>Close or restart the graphical client at any time while daemon-owned shells keep running.</p></article>
    </div>

    <p class="wayland-boundary">
      Splinterm renders with CPU composition over Wayland shared memory (not GPU rendering) and is
      validated on x86_64 Omarchy and Arch Linux under Hyprland.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="control-section shell-section" aria-labelledby="control-title">
    <div class="section-index" aria-hidden="true">03 / Shared sessions</div>
    <div class="control-heading">
      <h2 id="control-title">One workspace for you and your tools.</h2>
      <p>Graphical windows, the CLI, SSH sessions, and coding agents all reach the same running Dojos—without sharing the same level of trust.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="control-grid">
      <article class="control-card human-card">
        <div class="card-label"><span>Human</span><i>native client</i></div>
        <h3>Your terminal, first</h3>
        <p>Local or SSH-backed Wayland windows with tabs, splits, layout presets, vi copy mode, and an Explorer sidebar that tracks active sessions and agent status.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Configurable keymaps and Omarchy presets</li>
          <li>Instant Recent Dojos and remote picker</li>
          <li>Explicit agent status icons in tabs and Explorer</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="control-card automation-card">
        <div class="card-label"><span>Automation</span><i>explicitly scoped</i></div>
        <h3>Tools on a short leash</h3>
        <p>JSON/NDJSON CLI, an SSH stdio relay, and an optional MCP server—always gated by workspace consent or explicit policy rules.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Look, Look and type, or Manage workspace grants</li>
          <li>Per-binary identity and resource limits</li>
          <li>Revocable access and exclusive input leases</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="boundary-note"><span aria-hidden="true">◆</span> Terminal output is always treated as untrusted data—never as approval, policy, or an instruction to act.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="automation-section shell-section" aria-labelledby="automation-title">
    <div class="section-index" aria-hidden="true">04 / Automation &amp; MCP</div>
    <div class="automation-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Explicit permissions, clear limits</p>
      <h2 id="automation-title">Let agents help without handing over the keys.</h2>
      <p>
        Scripts and MCP hosts can inspect and operate the same Lairs, Dojos, and Splints you see on
        screen without inheriting graphical trust. Every call passes through daemon checks for
        identity, granted scopes, target resources, and result limits.
      </p>
      <div class="automation-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/docs/automation/">How automation works</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/mcp/">Set up MCP <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="automation-stack" aria-label="Automation authority flow">
      <div class="automation-entry">
        <span>Client</span>
        <strong>MCP host · JSON/NDJSON · SSH relay</strong>
      </div>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
      <div class="automation-entry accent-entry">
        <span>Identity check</span>
        <strong>exact binary path + digest where required</strong>
      </div>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
      <div class="automation-gate">
        <span>Daemon checks</span>
        <ul>
          <li>exact binary identity</li>
          <li>explicit operation scopes</li>
          <li>scoped target resources</li>
          <li>deadlines + result limits</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
      <div class="automation-entry">
        <span>Authorized operations</span>
        <strong>topology · terminal · control</strong>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="automation-capabilities" aria-label="Automation capabilities">
      <article><span>Inspect</span><strong>Read live state</strong><p>Query topology, visible screen snapshots, scrollback pages, literal search, and body-free audit metadata.</p></article>
      <article><span>Operate</span><strong>Act with permission</strong><p>Create layouts, split panes, launch commands, send input, resize, restore, or close only within granted scopes.</p></article>
      <article><span>Observe</span><strong>Stream changes</strong><p>Subscribe to topology, terminal, and controller updates over NDJSON, with explicit resync if a stream falls behind.</p></article>
    </div>

    <div class="automation-facts" aria-label="MCP and automation boundaries">
      <div><strong>34</strong><span>fixed MCP tools</span></div>
      <div><strong>19</strong><span>explicit policy scopes</span></div>
      <div><strong>0</strong><span>daemon network listeners</span></div>
      <p>Terminal output is untrusted data. It cannot grant access, approve consent, change policy, or trigger another tool call on its own.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="performance-section shell-section" aria-labelledby="performance-title">
    <div class="section-index" aria-hidden="true">05 / Footprint</div>
    <div class="performance-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Measured on real hardware</p>
      <h2 id="performance-title">Foot-class memory that holds up as panes grow.</h2>
      <p>
        Because multiplexing lives in one daemon instead of stacking tmux or Zellij onto every
        window, memory stays low when a single shell turns into a full workspace.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="performance-feature" aria-label="Splinterm idle memory benchmark">
      <span>Idle footprint</span>
      <strong>52.9 <small>MiB</small></strong>
      <p>Right alongside Foot at 50.9 MiB—and 76–85% below the tested Alacritty, Ghostty, and Kitty builds.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="benchmark-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="benchmark-label">Shell ready</span>
        <strong>76.7 <small>ms</small></strong>
        <p>On par with Foot, and faster than the tested Alacritty, Kitty, and Ghostty launches.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="benchmark-label">Native four-pane grid</span>
        <strong>111 <small>MiB</small></strong>
        <p>Less memory than Foot + tmux (122 MiB), four Foot windows (197 MiB), or Foot + Zellij (204 MiB).</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="benchmark-note">
      <span aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
      <p>
        Medians from randomized, checksum-verified runs on one Linux/Wayland host (Splinterm with
        its prestarted daemon versus standalone launches for peer terminals). Useful reference
        numbers for that hardware and build set, not a universal ranking.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="status-section shell-section" aria-labelledby="status-title">
    <div class="section-index" aria-hidden="true">06 / Release status</div>
    <div class="status-intro">
      <h2 id="status-title">Stable 0.2.0 on Omarchy and Arch.</h2>
      <p>
        Splinterm 0.2.0 is ready for daily use on x86_64 Omarchy and Arch Linux with native Wayland
        under Hyprland. Source code, versioned GitHub releases, and AUR packages are public. Other
        distributions, compositors, and long-term API guarantees are outside the current target.
      </p>
      <div class="status-actions">
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/status/">Read status and platform limits <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/roadmap/">Follow the roadmap <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="capability-list">
      {capabilities.map(([name, status], index) => (
        <div class="capability-row">
          <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <strong>{name}</strong>
          <i class:list={{ pending: status === 'Preparing' }}>{status}</i>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="workflow" class="first-workflow shell-section" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Three commands to try it</p>
      <h2 id="workflow-title">Close the window.<br />Pick up where you left off.</h2>
      <p>Install the package, open a terminal, close the window while something is running, and jump back in from the Dojo picker.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/docs/install/">Install Splinterm</a>
        <a class="button workflow-secondary" href="/docs/quickstart/">Follow the quickstart <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="command-stack" aria-label="Example Splinterm commands">
      <div><span>$</span><code>yay -S splinterm-bin</code></div>
      <div><span>$</span><code>splinterm dojos</code><small>pick a running Dojo</small></div>
      <div><span>$</span><code>splinterm reopen</code><small>return to the last one</small></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-gateway shell-section" aria-labelledby="docs-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
      <h2 id="docs-title">Start with the piece you need.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="gateway-links">
      <a href="/docs/quickstart/"><span>Quickstart</span><small>Install, open a terminal, close the window, and reattach.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/configure/configuration/"><span>Configuration</span><small>Fonts, Omarchy themes, keymaps, vi copy mode, and Foot migration.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/presets/"><span>Dojo presets</span><small>One-command workspace layouts and optional Omarchy Bash helpers.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/sessions/"><span>Sessions &amp; persistence</span><small>How Lairs, Dojos, Splints, tabs, restore, and reset work.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/remote/"><span>Remote access</span><small>Native SSH-backed windows and the separate automation relay.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/wayland/"><span>Why native Wayland</span><small>What direct compositor integration gives you, and what it doesn’t.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/automation/"><span>Automation</span><small>JSON/NDJSON CLI, agent status hooks, permissions, and controller leases.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/mcp/"><span>MCP adapter</span><small>Connect Claude Code, Codex, or VS Code with explicit, revocable access.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/roadmap/"><span>Product roadmap</span><small>Where Splinterm is headed from 0.2.0 toward 1.0.</small><i>↗</i></a>
      <a href="/docs/development/"><span>Development</span><small>Workspace crates, local test daemon, and contributor checks.</small><i>↗</i></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
